  "evidence": "TEXT_E: Leave the single market and customs union; Great Repeal Bill; reduce net migration and keep students in migration stats; maintain defence spending at ≥2% of GDP and retain the nuclear deterrent; minimum £8bn NHS real-terms increase with expanded mental health workforce; keep taxes low and raise personal allowance; energy tariff cap; build 1.5m homes. TEXT_D: Hold a referendum on the final EU deal with an option to remain and seek close EU ties; add 1p to Income Tax for NHS/social care; remove students from migration stats; extensive constitutional reforms (STV, votes at 16, Lords reform); strong green agenda including banning new diesel car/small van sales by 2025.",
  "reasoning": "Voters’ top concerns in this period centered on delivering Brexit, the NHS, immigration control, the economy/cost of living, and security, which TEXT_E addresses directly and prominently with clear commitments on leaving the EU, reducing migration, funding the NHS, keeping taxes low/energy caps, and strengthening defence. TEXT_D emphasizes a second EU referendum and constitutional/environmental reforms that were less salient to the median voter, despite also proposing NHS funding changes.",
